w: 1.~Give ear un- to me when I call, God of my right- eous- ness: Have mer- cy, hear my pray'r; thou hast en- larg'd me in dis- tress.
w: 2.~O ye the sons of men! how long will ye love va- ni- ties? How long my glo- ry turn to shame, and will ye fol- low lies?
w: 3.~But know, that for him- self the Lord the god- ly man doth chuse: The Lord, when I on him do call, to hear will not re- fuse.
w: 4.~Fear, and sin not; talk with your heart on bed, and si- lent be. Off 'rings pre- sent of right- eous- ness, and in the Lord trust ye.
w: 5.~O who will shew us a- ny good? is that which ma- ny say: But of thy coun- ten- ance the light, Lord, lift on us al- way.
w: 6.~Up- on my heart, be- stow'd by thee, more glad- ness I have found Than they, ev'n then, when corn and wine did most with them a- bound.
w: 7.~I will both lay me down in peace, and qui- et sleep will take; Be- cause thou on- ly me to dwell in safe- ty, Lord, dost make.
